{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEven with schema-filtered selection (the selection story this depends on), extraction from\nfree text stays heuristic: a trailing schema-valid decoy can win, and models fence-wrap quoted\nJSON so fences cannot serve as the channel (measured: E2 catch 2, experiment-results comment\non the selection bug). An explicit output channel makes the payload location a stated contract\ninstead of an inference.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtend `schema_directive` (src/rebar/llm/structured.py:63) to instruct the model to wrap its\nsingle final JSON object between the literal markers `<<<REBAR_OUTPUT>>>` and `<<<END>>>`.\nExtraction in `parse_structured` (:212) tries marker-delimited blocks FIRST — the LAST marker\nblock wins, validated via `validate_to` (:177) — and falls back to the selection pipeline for\nmarkerless replies, so older transcripts and non-compliant models degrade gracefully.\n\nThe directive literal is SINGLE-SOURCED as a module constant in structured.py (e.g.\n`SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE`, naming both markers) so exactly one string is used by `schema_directive`,\nby the marker extractor, and by the measurement harness — no drift between what the model is told\nand what the parser looks for. The a40f harness currently appends a PLACEHOLDER directive\n(tests/external/test_structured_output_matrix.py `_SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE`, whose own comment says\n\"the sentinel story (a sibling) owns refining it\"); this story replaces that placeholder with the\nreal constant so the operator-attested before/after run (AC4) measures THIS story's actual\ndirective against the current parser, not a prototype.\n\nDirective and extraction land in ONE change, deliberately: E2 measured that shipping the\ndirective alone flips quote-in-prose from 0% to 100% and breaks the current parser 10/10 — a\nsplit would leave the system broken between changes. The marker literal is the E2-tested one\n(16/16 compliance across sonnet/haiku on Bedrock, 0/26 decoy wins). Prior art: Anthropic's\nanswer-tag extraction guidance and aider's `<<<`-style sentinel blocks; markdown fences are\ndisqualified as the channel because models fence-wrap quoted material (E2). The directive is\nappended to the user prompt at src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:202 (`schema_hint`), which is\ncache-prefix-neutral; that call site does not change.\n\n## Scope\n\n- src/rebar/llm/structured.py — the single-sourced `SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE` constant, its inclusion\n  in `schema_directive`, and marker extraction in `parse_structured` (tried BEFORE the\n  schema-filtered selection pipeline; last marker block wins; markerless replies fall through\n  byte-for-byte to today's selection path)\n- tests/external/test_structured_output_matrix.py — replace the placeholder `_SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE`\n  with the single-sourced constant so the harness measures this story's real directive\n- tests/unit/test_structured.py — marker pins, incl. the fenced-quote hazard pin\n- tests/fixtures/ — E2 sentinel replies added to the corpus\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit/contract tier (real reply text through the real parser):\n\n- Marker extraction pins: one block; multiple blocks -> LAST wins; markerless -> selection\n  fallback yields the identical result (parameterized contrast against the selection story's\n  tests); marker block containing a fenced object -> payload still wins.\n- The fenced-quote hazard pin: a fence-wrapped schema-decoy plus a marker-wrapped payload ->\n  the marker payload wins (regression pin for E2 catch 2).\n- Marker-first ordering pin: a reply carrying BOTH a marker-wrapped valid payload AND an\n  earlier schema-valid decoy object parses to the MARKER payload (markers beat the selection\n  pipeline). Mutation check: dropping marker priority (extraction ignores markers, or runs the\n  selection pipeline first) turns this and the E2-reply corpus test RED.\n- Markerless regression pin: a reply with NO markers yields the IDENTICAL result the\n  schema-filtered selection pipeline produces today (byte-for-byte fall-through), so df3a-era and\n  non-compliant-model transcripts are unaffected.\n- LLM-surface evidence is measured by the harness story (test-design §10: paired directives,\n  fixed n, deterministic scoring) and recorded here as an operator-attested artifact.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The directive names both markers and extraction honors them with last-block-wins —\n      checked by the new unit pins in tests/unit/test_structured.py.\n- [ ] A markerless reply falls back to schema-filtered selection with identical results —\n      checked by a parameterized contrast test.\n- [ ] A fence-wrapped decoy alongside a marker-wrapped payload parses to the payload — checked\n      by the pinned hazard test.\n- [ ] A reply carrying a marker-wrapped valid payload AND an EARLIER plain schema-valid decoy\n      object parses to the marker payload (markers beat the schema-filtered selection pipeline,\n      not merely fences) — checked by the marker-first ordering pin in tests/unit/test_structured.py.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] A harness before/after run (old vs sentinel directive) shows parse\n      success at or above the predeclared threshold (>= 95% per cell) with the artifact link\n      recorded as a comment on this ticket."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785917112296373001,"uuid":"9868ccd4-8b64-49fa-b97a-151b41a19ac4"}